WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:25.670
<v Bim Adewunmi>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Bim Adewunmi, in for Ira Glass. My younger cousin, Kamyl, is not really a dog person, but there is one dog she adored. Her name was Foxy, because she looked exactly like a fox, except she was black. She was the neighbor's dog, but she and Kamyl seemed to have a real kinship, maybe because they both weren't very far from the ground.
<v Bim Adewunmi>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是Bim Adewunmi，代班Ira Glass。我的小表妹Kamyl并不是特别喜欢狗，但她曾经非常崇拜一只狗。那只狗叫Foxy，因为它长得像狐狸，除了它是黑色的。那是邻居家的狗，但它和Kamyl之间似乎有一种真正的亲缘关系，也许是因为它们俩离地面的距离都不远。

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:45.400
<v Bim Adewunmi>Kamyl was around four or five years old back then, and she had a little lisp, so "Foxy" came out as "Fozhy." I thought it was one of the cutest things I'd ever heard. The way Kamyl remembers Foxy, it's almost like a movie. Her memories feel like endless summer, hazy and perfect, like a scene shot on crackly film.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Kamyl那时大约四五岁，说话还有点口齿不清，所以把“Foxy”说成了“Fozhy”。我觉得那是我听过的最可爱的事情之一。Kamyl记忆中的Foxy，就像一部电影。她的记忆感觉像无尽的夏天，朦胧而完美，就像在带有杂音的胶片上拍摄的一幕。

00:00:45.400 --> 00:01:02.380
<v Kamyl>I just remember the feeling of being excited to go and see Foxy. I have an image in my head of coming to the house, and I could see Foxy was outside. But I can see Foxy through the door that leads to the garden.
<v Kamyl>我只记得去见Foxy时那种兴奋的感觉。我的脑海里有一个画面：我来到那栋房子，可以看到Foxy在外面。但我可以通过通往花园的那扇门看到它。

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:24.430
<v Bim Adewunmi>There's a story about Kamyl and Foxy that I think about fairly often. I've talked about it with my sister for years, but never with Kamyl. And it's this. Once when they were playing, Foxy nipped at Kamyl, nothing serious, just an excited kid playing with an excited dog. One minute they were playing, half under the coffee table in the middle of the room. And the next, they weren't.
<v Bim Adewunmi>关于Kamyl和Foxy，有一个我经常想起的故事。我和我姐姐谈论过很多年，但从未和Kamyl提起过。事情是这样的：有一次它们在玩耍时，Foxy咬了Kamyl一口，没什么大碍，只是一个兴奋的孩子在和一个兴奋的狗玩。上一刻它们还在房间中间的咖啡桌下玩耍，下一刻它们就不在那里了。

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:42.320
<v Bim Adewunmi>There was no need to go to the hospital or anything, but it was enough to startle Kamyl and to make her cry. But it's what happened after that I think about the most. The day after Foxy nipped her, Kamyl went right back to playing with the dog, but she would not go back to the place where the nip happened.
<v Bim Adewunmi>虽然没必要去医院或做别的什么，但这足以让Kamyl受惊并哭泣。但最让我挂心的是之后发生的事。Foxy咬她后的第二天，Kamyl又回去和那只狗玩了，但她死活不肯回到那个被咬的地方。

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:58.030
<v Bim Adewunmi>If her feet unthinkingly moved her towards the coffee table, she'd redirect. She'd swerve her little body around it. Instead of avoiding the dog, she steered clear of the place. When I brought this up to Kamyl, she had no memory of doing it.
<v Bim Adewunmi>如果她的脚不假思索地朝咖啡桌走去，她就会绕开。她会用她那小小的身体绕过它。她没有避开那只狗，而是避开了那个地方。当我向Kamyl提起这件事时，她根本不记得自己这样做过。

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.420
<v Kamyl>Did anybody try-- did they test me?
<v Kamyl>有没有人试过——他们测试过我吗？

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:01.870
<v Bim Adewunmi>Yes.
<v Bim Adewunmi>有的。

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.420
<v Kamyl>Did they put a treat in that area and I just would not go there?
<v Kamyl>他们是不是在那个区域放了零食，然后我就是不肯过去？

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.770
<v Bim Adewunmi>I mean, you're talking like you're the dog. [LAUGHS]
<v Bim Adewunmi>我的意思是，你说话就像你自己是那只狗一样。[笑]

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:18.520
<v Kamyl>Yeah, I guess. Did anyone sit me down to talk about, oh, Kamyl, why don't you go to that corner?
<v Kamyl>是啊，我想也是。有没有人让我坐下来谈谈，问问“Kamyl，你为什么不去那个角落？”

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:38.050
<v Bim Adewunmi>I don't know. That suggests that we were mature enough to try and help you solve the problem. I think we just found it funny that you avoided the area. I think we were all kind of like, watch this. Watch this. And then we'd try and get you to come around, and then we'd laugh when you wouldn't, which is very cruel, in hindsight. I'm so sorry.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我不知道。那说明我们当时还没成熟到能帮你解决问题。我想我们只是觉得你避开那个区域很有趣。我觉得我们当时都有点像是在看戏，说“看着，看着”，然后我们会试着让你走过来，当你拒绝时我们就笑，事后想来，这真的很残忍。我很抱歉。

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:52.570
<v Bim Adewunmi>And even more curious, Kamyl still seems to have a hard time fathoming this, that Foxy, her beloved friend, could even unwittingly hurt her. 20-ish years later, she still won't let herself go there.
<v Bim Adewunmi>更奇怪的是，Kamyl似乎仍然无法理解这一点：Foxy，她心爱的朋友，竟然会无意中伤害她。大约20年后，她仍然不允许自己去那个地方。

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:59.400
<v Kamyl>A part of me is like-- you know African parents. They like to overexaggerate. She probably just licked me.
<v Kamyl>我的一部分心声是——你知道非洲父母。他们喜欢夸大其词。她可能只是舔了我一下。

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:11.750
<v Bim Adewunmi>She didn't lick you. [LAUGHS] She didn't lick you. She definitely nipped you, but I love how-- you're doing it again. You're recasting the past. You're like, no. Did she? She did.
<v Bim Adewunmi>她没舔你。[笑] 她没舔你。她绝对是咬了你，但我喜欢你这样——你又在做了。你正在重塑过去。你在想，不，她是吗？她是这样做的。

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.460
<v Bim Adewunmi>Because it's weird. It's weird that I don't remember it. It would be different if I'm like, I don't remember it.
<v Bim Adewunmi>因为这很奇怪。我不记得这件事真的很奇怪。如果我说“我不记得了”，那情况会不一样。

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:38.660
<v Bim Adewunmi>Avoidance is a surprisingly common reaction to information that might knock us off kilter, shying away from the things we cannot wrap our minds around. It's self-preservation. The world is so full of dangerous things, things that might hurt us if we look too closely at them. So we avoid. We circumvent. We choose not to go there.
<v Bim Adewunmi>回避是一种出人意料的常见反应，当我们面对可能让我们不知所措的信息时，我们往往会避开那些无法理解的事情。这是一种自我保护。世界充满了危险的事物，如果看得太仔细，可能会伤害到我们。所以我们回避。我们绕道。我们选择不去那里。

00:03:38.660 --> 00:04:10.450
<v Bim Adewunmi>That's our show today in three acts, people finding there's one place in their lives they simply can't access for reasons that, sometimes, even they don't fully understand. There's just a mental "keep out" sign on the door, do not look. Do not pass go. Do not collect $200. Stay with us.
<v Bim Adewunmi>这就是我们今天的节目，分为三幕，讲述人们发现生命中有一个地方他们无法触及，原因有时连他们自己也不完全理解。就像门上挂着一块写着“禁止进入”的心理告示，不要看。不要通过。不要领取200美元。留下来吧。

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.340
<v Bim Adewunmi>I Coulda Grown Big in Japan.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我也许能在日本长大。

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:29.150
<v Bim Adewunmi>Atsuko Okatsuka is a comedian, and she's incredibly close with her grandma, Ying-Hsi. They talk several times a day. They're together every weekend. And Atsuko makes these incredible videos of herself dancing, while her grandmother plays incidental hype woman.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Atsuko Okatsuka是一位喜剧演员，她和她的祖母Ying-Hsi关系极其亲密。她们一天要通好几次话。她们每个周末都在一起。Atsuko还会制作那些令人惊叹的自舞视频，而她的祖母则在一旁扮演那种即兴的捧场角色。

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:40.480
<v Bim Adewunmi>Her grandma raised her, but for a while now, Atsuko has joked on stage that her grandmother stole her from her dad when she was little and brought her and her mother from Japan to live in the US.
<v Bim Adewunmi>是祖母把她养大的，但有一段时间，Atsuko在台上开玩笑说，她小时候祖母把她从爸爸身边偷走了，并带她和她母亲从日本搬到美国生活。

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:47.450
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>From the outside, she looks like this sweet, unassuming old Asian woman, but she's a liar.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>从外表看，她看起来像个甜美、谦逊的亚洲老太太，但她是个骗子。

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.560
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[LAUGHTER]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[笑声]

00:04:49.560 --> 00:05:13.170
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>She a liar. When I was eight, she told me we were coming to the States for a two-month vacation, so I packed lightly. And then we never left. Yeah, true story, yeah, yeah. And till this day, we're still dealing with the kidnapping in our own ways.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>她是个骗子。我八岁时，她告诉我我们要来美国度两周假，所以我打包得很轻便。然后我们就再也没离开过。是的，真实的故事。直到今天，我们仍然在以自己的方式处理这场绑架事件。

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:31.030
<v Bim Adewunmi>But the truth is, Atsuko only has the sketchiest idea of what really happened. She doesn't really know how she ended up in America. Her family doesn't discuss it. Talking about it has been a no-go zone for her and her family. But recently, Atsuko decided to find out.
<v Bim Adewunmi>但事实是，Atsuko对到底发生了什么只有极其模糊的概念。她并不真正知道自己是如何来到美国的。她的家人不讨论这件事。谈论这件事一直是她和她家人之间的禁区。但最近，Atsuko决定一探究竟。

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:57.340
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Because my family doesn't talk about this, as a kid, all I knew was I was living in Japan, and suddenly I wasn't. I was separated from my dad and my friends. I was forced to go to school in America and learn English and start a new life. I went by "Stacey" for two months. It was jarring and confusing, and I blamed my dad for giving up on us and leaving us in America.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>因为我的家人不谈论这件事，小时候我只知道我住在日本，突然间就不是了。我与爸爸和朋友们分开了。我被迫去美国的学校学习英语，开始新的生活。我用了两个月叫“Stacey”的名字。那真是令人震惊和困惑，我责怪爸爸放弃了我们，把我们留在美国。

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:26.040
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Then, as an adult, that shifted. I decided my grandma was the bad guy. She stole me away from my dad, and he was the victim. But I never asked anybody the hard questions to find out the actual truth. It was way easier to just talk about it on stage, where nobody could fight you on whether it's real or not. That's why I love standup. Nobody talks back to you. It's good for people who don't want to process feelings.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>后来，成年后，这种想法转变了。我认定祖母是坏人。她把我从爸爸身边偷走了，而他是受害者。但我从未问过任何人那些尖锐的问题来弄清真相。在舞台上谈论这件事容易得多，没人能反驳你它是真是假。这就是我喜欢单口喜剧的原因。没人会对你回嘴。这对那些不想处理情绪的人来说很好。

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:57.640
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>And then my dad had a stroke. He survived it and is recovering, but it was kind of a wake-up call. Am I really going to never ask what happened with my move to the United States? I mean, it was the biggest event in my life. It completely changed everything. Time to get answers. Was I kidnapped? What really happened?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>然后我爸爸中风了。他活了下来并正在康复，但这对我来说是一个警钟。难道我真的永远不去问我搬到美国时到底发生了什么吗？我的意思是，那是我生命中最大的事件。它彻底改变了一切。是时候寻找答案了。我被绑架了吗？到底发生了什么？

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:02.200
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I'm recording. Test, test. OK. OK. Oh, Grandma--
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我在录音。测试，测试。好吧。好吧。噢，祖母——

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:11.170
<v Bim Adewunmi>So I started with my grandma, the perpetrator, and confronted her face to face. She and my mom lived just 20 minutes away.
<v Bim Adewunmi>所以我从我的祖母——那个始作俑者——开始，和她面对面摊牌。她和妈妈就住在20分钟路程外。

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.790
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Did you kidnap me?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>你绑架我了吗？

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:13.390
<v Ying-Hsi Li>Oh, no.
<v Ying-Hsi Li>噢，没有。

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:14.920
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Did you kidnap me?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>你绑架我了吗？

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:15.370
<v Ying-Hsi Li>No.
<v Ying-Hsi Li>没有。

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:21.400
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>That's what I tell audiences. When I perform I say, yeah, my grandma pretty much kidnapped me.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>这就是我告诉观众的。我表演时会说，是的，我祖母基本就是绑架了我。

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:22.840
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:26.710
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[LAUGHS] [SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[笑][说普通话]

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:31.170
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>She's saying in Mandarin, "There's no such thing. I didn't kidnap you."
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>她说的是普通话，“没有那种事。我没有绑架你。”

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:36.520
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:44.980
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>"When people kidnap a child, they demand money, ransom to return the child. But we didn't ask for that, so it doesn't count as kidnapping."
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>“当人们绑架孩子时，他们会索要钱财，勒索赎金来换回孩子。但我们没要那个，所以那不算绑架。”

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:50.170
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:07:50.170 --> 00:08:13.560
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Oh, OK. She didn't ask for ransom. She's still a kidnapper, she's just bad at it. She took a kid to raise for free. Can you imagine trying to negotiate with a kidnapper, and you're like, please, how much money do you want? I just want my kid back. And they're like, oh, no. It's on me. Free. In fact, I'll take her to LA for you. I think she's going to be a star.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>噢，好吧。她没要赎金。她还是个绑架犯，只是手法很烂。她免费带走一个孩子来抚养。你能想象和一个绑架犯谈判吗？你就像说，求你了，你要多少钱？我只想换回我的孩子。然后他们说，噢，不。算我的。免费。实际上，我还帮你把她带到洛杉矶。我觉得她会成为明星的。

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:24.660
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I'm glad my grandma and I can joke about these things today. We sat on a couch in her living room. I told her, I think our family has a habit of not speaking about difficult things.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我很高兴我和祖母今天能拿这些事开玩笑。我们坐在她客厅的沙发上。我告诉她，我觉得我们家有一种不谈论困难事情的习惯。

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:31.590
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:36.720
<v Bim Adewunmi>As we talk, my mom interrupts us from the top of the stairs. My mom lives with her.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我们谈话时，妈妈从楼梯上打断了我们。妈妈和她住在一起。

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.480
<v Mom>You guys need to go away.
<v Mom>你们需要离开。

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:40.260
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>OK, Mom.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>好的，妈妈。

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.200
<v Mom>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Mom>[说普通话]

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.260
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>She's asking my grandmother to please not talk about these sad things. It'll affect how I see my life.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>她正求祖母不要谈论这些伤心事。这会影响我看待自己生活的方式。

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.710
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:55.110
<v Bim Adewunmi>"It won't. I asked her," I yelled.
<v Bim Adewunmi>“不会的。我问过她了，”我大喊道。

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:59.220
<v Mom>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Mom>[说普通话]

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.210
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>"Don't say it," she says. "Try not to talk about anything too sad."
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>“别说了，”她说。“尽量不要谈论太伤心的事情。”

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:04.950
<v Mom>Be happy.
<v Mom>开心点。

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:09.180
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>It's good to talk about the past.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>谈论过去是件好事。

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:23.650
<v Bim Adewunmi>My mom goes back to her room. So a big reason why we don't talk about things is because of my mom. She has paranoid schizophrenia, and talking about sad things can trigger bad memories and the voices in her head.
<v Bim Adewunmi>妈妈回到了她的房间。所以我们不谈论事情的一个很大原因是妈妈。她患有偏执型精神分裂症，谈论伤心事会触发不好的回忆和她脑海中的声音。

00:09:23.650 --> 00:10:00.610
<v Bim Adewunmi>So any time we want to talk about the past, we have to tiptoe around her. We whisper or just avoid it completely. And that's how our family has always done things. We didn't even talk about how my mom has schizophrenia. As a kid, my grandma would just say, oh, your mom just has low blood sugar. She's hungry. When she was clearly wilding out, throwing plates at Grandma. It's like, uh, she needs professional help not bread.
<v Bim Adewunmi>所以任何时候如果我们想谈论过去，都必须小心翼翼。我们要么耳语，要么完全避开它。这就是我们家一直以来的做法。我们甚至没有谈论过妈妈患有精神分裂症的事。小时候，祖母只会说，噢，你妈妈只是低血糖。她饿了。明明她当时在疯狂发作，向祖母扔盘子。这就像，呃，她需要专业治疗而不是面包。

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:23.800
<v Bim Adewunmi>I was eager to hear my dad's side of this. For starters, when they divorced, did he have custody of me? Because if he did, this seems way more like a real kidnapping. And did he even know that my grandma was taking me to the US? We talk, like, once a month. We reconnected when I was 15. So I flew to Japan to see him.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我很渴望听到爸爸那一边的说法。首先，他们离婚时，他有我的监护权吗？因为如果有的话，这看起来更像是一场真正的绑架。他甚至知道祖母带我来美国吗？我们大约一个月通一次话。我们在我15岁时重新建立了联系。所以我飞去日本看他。

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:31.240
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>So we just came from my dad's house. Now we're at my elementary school. This is where I would go to school.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我们刚从爸爸家出来。现在我们在我的小学。这就是我以前上学的地方。

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:41.950
<v Bim Adewunmi>When my dad and I see each other, we try to do as much as we can. He's up for anything, like getting on a big pink dog seesaw with me at the playground to shoot a little video.
<v Bim Adewunmi>当我和爸爸见面时，我们尽量多做些事。他什么都愿意尝试，比如在操场上和我一起坐上那个巨大的粉色狗狗跷跷板拍个小视频。

