00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:23.350
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>Oh, I understand your dad, because as a parent, it's impossible not to want to see your child. So we empathize with him, but our feelings were complex.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>噢，我理解你爸爸，因为作为家长，不可能不想见自己的孩子。所以我们同情他，但我们的感情很复杂。

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:28.280
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.070
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I told her, "You know I really like my dad, right?"
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我告诉她，“你知道我真的很喜欢我爸爸，对吧？”

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.770
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:41.180
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>I suppose so, because you were a kind kid, but for us, it was complicated. We were scared that he would take you away.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>我想是吧，因为你是个善良的孩子，但对我们来说，情况很复杂。我们害怕他会带走你。

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:44.060
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:22:44.060 --> 00:23:00.270
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>Also, your mom still resented your dad. That's why she didn't want you to see him, so it became like this.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>而且，你妈妈仍然怨恨你爸爸。这就是为什么她不想让你见他，所以就变成了这样。

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:12.400
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>It went on like this for a year or two, and my grandma was realizing how hard it was to hold everything together. And my mom was getting a lot worse. Grandma says the divorce really changed something in her.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>这种情况持续了一两年，祖母意识到要维持一切有多难。妈妈的情况越来越糟。祖母说离婚确实改变了她。

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:15.950
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:22.440
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>It really crushed her. There was a period of time when she wanted to hide, and she didn't want to see anyone.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>这彻底摧毁了她。有一段时间她想躲起来，不想见任何人。

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:25.590
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:55.290
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>What's strange is, I actually don't remember much of my mom from when we were in Japan. I feel like there's memories of me doing things with you, whether we're bicycling outdoors, you taking me to dance classes, me going to the park with you and my friends and then, separately, me doing things with my dad. But I don't remember my mom being in the picture so much. What was she doing? What was her daily schedule like?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>奇怪的是，我其实不记得多少在日本时妈妈的事。我觉得我有和你们一起做事的记忆，无论是户外骑行，你带我去上舞蹈课，我和你及朋友去公园，然后，分开地，我做和爸爸在一起的事。但我不太记得妈妈在画面里。她在做什么？她的日常生活是什么样的？

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:58.470
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:01.240
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>She was just staying home all day.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>她只是整天待在家里。

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:05.140
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>And just be alone? And she had no friends, right?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>然后一个人待着？她没有朋友，对吧？

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:06.880
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:09.000
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>Yes.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>是的。

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:22.970
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>But how bad did my mom get? I remember the night where she did take out the kitchen knife and maybe threatened to harm herself or us. But what exactly happened that night, again?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>但我妈妈情况变得多糟？我记得那天晚上她确实拿出了菜刀，可能威胁要伤害自己或我们。但那天晚上到底发生了什么？

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:30.780
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:51.090
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>There was one time late at night she had a knife in her hand, and it looked like she might want to attack us. I was scared, so I got you out of the house on a bike, and we went around the neighborhood. We biked and biked around the neighborhood till very late, before we went home.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>有一次深夜她手里拿着刀，看起来她可能想攻击我们。我很害怕，所以我骑自行车带你离开了家，我们在附近转悠。我们骑啊骑，转了很久，直到很晚才回家。

00:24:51.090 --> 00:25:09.380
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I mean, my mother would never hurt me. She says that I'm all she has, but that scared my grandma. It put her on high alert. But she couldn't ask my dad for help, because she knew that would upset my mom. So she took charge, like always, and made a big decision.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我的意思是，我母亲永远不会伤害我。她说我是她拥有的一切，但那吓坏了祖母。这让她高度警觉。但她不能向爸爸求助，因为她知道那会让我妈难过。所以她像往常一样负责，并做了一个重大决定。

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:17.880
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:32.190
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>I felt like I couldn't protect you and your mom on my own, but if we move to the US, I've got the help of my son and my daughter-in-law. So I decided to move all of us to the US.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>我觉得我无法独自保护你和你妈妈，但如果我们搬到美国，我有我儿子和儿媳的帮忙。所以我决定带我们所有人搬到美国。

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:55.960
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I feel so much for my grandma in this moment. I didn't know she moved us to America because she was scared for me, that she was feeling so alone and overwhelmed. But I also feel for my dad. Did he know we were moving? And if he did, why didn't he do anything about it? This is one of the things I most wanted to ask my dad about.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>这一刻我非常同情我的祖母。我不知道她带我们搬到美国是因为她为我担心，她感到如此孤独和不知所措。但我同样同情我的父亲。他知道我们要搬走吗？如果他知道，他为什么没做点什么？这是我最想问爸爸的事情之一。

00:25:55.960 --> 00:26:00.590
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I did not know we were moving to America. Did you know?
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我不知道我们要搬到美国。你知道吗？

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:03.330
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:07.720
<v Dad Through Interpreter>Did I know from the very beginning? No, I didn't know.
<v Dad Through Interpreter>我从一开始就知道吗？不，我不知道。

00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:14.590
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Grandma confirmed this. She didn't tell him, not before we left. He realized it later.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>祖母证实了这一点。她没告诉他，在我们离开之前。他后来才意识到。

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:19.510
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:35.230
<v Dad Through Interpreter>Right now, I'm almost about to cry a little. So basically, your grandma told me in a letter that you are really missing me, and that's when I think I knew you were in America. I really wanted to see you, too.
<v Dad Through Interpreter>现在，我几乎要哭出来了。所以基本上，你祖母在信里告诉我你真的很想我，那时我想我就知道你在美国了。我也真的很想见你。

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:38.940
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:26:38.940 --> 00:27:18.620
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I have some feelings of guilt that, as a child, because I love both Mom and Dad and Grandma, I love everyone so much. So sometimes I fear that in the past, maybe I hurt your feelings if I said I wanted to go with Mom and Grandma. But I want you to know that I was just a confused kid who wanted to see everyone equally, so I want you to know that I always, always wanted to be with you, too.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我有种负罪感，作为孩子，因为我爱妈妈、爸爸和祖母，我爱每个人。所以有时我担心过去，如果我说我想和妈妈及祖母在一起，可能会伤害你的感情。但我希望你知道我只是一个困惑的孩子，想平等地看到每个人，所以我想让你知道我一直、一直也想和你在一起。

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:33.590
<v Bim Adewunmi>Then my dad told me something that completely shattered everything I thought I knew going into this. He said that back then, he thought maybe it was better for me to be with my mom and grandma. He was working all the time, and my siblings were mean to me.
<v Bim Adewunmi>然后爸爸告诉我的一件事彻底粉碎了我所知道的一切。他说，那时他认为也许让我和妈妈及祖母在一起对我更好。他一直在工作，而我的兄弟姐妹对我不好。

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:56.490
<v Bim Adewunmi>He felt he couldn't provide the best environment for me. And here were my mom and grandma, so excited to have me. Maybe I should be with them, he thought. That's why he didn't fight to keep me. This was all news to me.
<v Bim Adewunmi>他觉得他不能给我最好的环境。而妈妈和祖母，她们如此兴奋地拥有我。也许我应该和她们在一起，他想。这就是他没为留住我而战的原因。这些对我来说都是新消息。

00:27:56.490 --> 00:28:00.470
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:05.370
<v Dad Through Interpreter>So I've never gotten angry about it but, I felt disappointed, naturally.
<v Dad Through Interpreter>所以我从没对此感到生气，但，自然地，我感到失望。

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:08.950
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:27.970
<v Dad Through Interpreter>I kind of felt it can't be helped. Your mom and grandma felt that even though I had custody, when they saw you, they just loved you so much that they wanted to be with you. And so they took you with them. I think those feelings are natural, so I didn't get angry.
<v Dad Through Interpreter>我有点觉得那无法避免。你妈妈和你祖母觉得，尽管我有监护权，但当她们看到你，她们真的很爱你，她们想和你在一起。所以她们带走了你。我觉得这些感受很自然，所以我没生气。

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:30.070
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:28:30.070 --> 00:28:35.920
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I admire how much you have empathy for other people.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我很钦佩你有多少对他人的同理心。

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:39.520
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:50.650
<v Dad Through Interpreter>Thank you. Thank you, Atsuko. So right now, you are praising me. But if I really understood people's feelings, I wouldn't have made so many mistakes in my life.
<v Dad Through Interpreter>谢谢。谢谢你，Atsuko。所以现在，你在称赞我。但如果我真的理解人们的感受，我就不会在生活中犯这么多错误了。

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:54.300
<v Dad>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Dad>[日语]

00:28:54.300 --> 00:29:12.590
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>It's interesting that my dad thought he was looking out for my best interests by letting me go. It broke his heart and mine, too. But he did it for me. He could have confronted my grandma, just like my grandma could have told him the truth about taking me to America.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>有趣的是，爸爸以为放我走是为我好。这让他心碎，也让我心碎。但他确实是为了我。他本可以面对我的祖母，就像我祖母本可以告诉他关于带我来美国的真相一样。

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:50.630
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>But this is one way they're similar. They avoid these conversations, because when you converse, there's conflict. And my family, we don't like that. They could have done it, though, and they could have talked to me. I would have asked to go back to Japan. I would have asked to see both my parents equally. They could figure out the details from there, summers in America, school year in Japan, maybe. Who knows? They're the adults. I was eight years old.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>但这是他们相似的一点。他们回避这些谈话，因为当谈话时，会有冲突。而我们家，不喜欢那样。不过他们本可以那样做，本可以和我谈谈。我会要求回日本。我会要求平等地见我的父母。他们可以从那里解决细节，暑假在美国，学期在日本，也许吧。谁知道呢？他们是成年人。我当时八岁。

00:29:50.630 --> 00:30:25.740
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Before we left for America, my friends from second grade in Japan threw a farewell party for me. But I thought it was just them being dramatic, because I was coming back. I liked the attention, though. We were at a McDonald's, and everyone brought me gifts. They also wrote me tons of letters, too, that I discovered years later in a box. They all said they were going to miss me. So pretty much all of Chiba knew I was leaving for good, except for me and my dad. It's like The Truman Show directed by my grandma.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>在我们去美国之前，我在日本二年级的朋友为我举办了告别派对。但我以为只是她们在装模作样，因为我会回来的。但我喜欢那种关注。我们在麦当劳，每个人都给我送礼物。她们还写了大量的信给我，我多年后在箱子里发现了。她们都说会想我。所以基本上整个千叶都知道我永远离开了，除了我和我爸爸。就像我祖母导演的《楚门的世界》。

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:34.760
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:30:34.760 --> 00:30:44.480
<v Bim Adewunmi>I told her, "When you brought me here without telling me or my dad, you knew we'd find out one day, right? Did you think about that?"
<v Bim Adewunmi>我告诉她，“当你带我来这里却没告诉我或爸爸时，你知道我们终有一天会发现的，对吧？你想过那个吗？”

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:47.930
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:30:47.930 --> 00:30:52.600
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>I guess I thought, whatever happens, happens. I just let everything unfold.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>我想我当时想的是，无论发生什么，就发生吧。我只是让一切顺其自然。

00:30:52.600 --> 00:31:17.690
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>I love that when it comes to intense things, my grandma's got a surfer attitude about it. She's all, ride the wave, bruh. It's just America. But this is how she generally deals with stuff in life. She raised three kids on her own after her husband died, and she just pushes ahead and figures something will work out. It has to.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>我喜欢我祖母在面对强烈的事情时有一种冲浪者的态度。她就是，去顺应潮流吧，哥们。只是美国而已。但这就是她处理生活事务的方式。她在丈夫去世后独自抚养了三个孩子，她只是勇往直前，觉得总会有办法的。必须如此。

00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:35.320
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>For years, she supported her kids by teaching English in Taiwan, without really speaking English. It was just another one of the scams she pulled. With a smile on her face and a few lies, she always figured it out. That's what she did with me.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>多年来，她通过在台湾教英语来供养她的孩子，而她并不真正会说英语。这只是她耍的另一个把戏。带着脸上的微笑和一点谎言，她总是能搞定。这就是她对我做的事。

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:42.480
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:48.450
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>I was worried that you would feel upset, so I had to trick you. Sorry. I'm sorry.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>我担心你会难过，所以我不得不骗你。抱歉。我很抱歉。

00:31:48.450 --> 00:31:52.920
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>So you lied, and [SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>所以你撒谎了，而且 [说普通话]

00:31:52.920 --> 00:32:10.560
<v Bim Adewunmi>When summer ended and she enrolled me in school in Los Angeles, that's when I realized we weren't going home. I started dreaming of being back in Japan, being with my friends, being with my dad. I had that dream every night.
<v Bim Adewunmi>当夏天结束，她给我报名洛杉矶的学校时，我才意识到我们不会回家了。我开始梦见回到日本，和朋友在一起，和爸爸在一起。我每晚都做那个梦。

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:15.400
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:18.810
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>I didn't know about this. I'm sorry. I didn't know.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>我不知道这件事。我很抱歉。我不知道。

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:23.250
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:30.520
<v Bim Adewunmi>I asked, "Did I cry or ask why we're still here? Did I tell you I wanted to see my dad?
<v Bim Adewunmi>我问，“我有没有哭或问为什么我们还在这里？我有没有告诉你我想见爸爸？”

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:37.210
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:43.960
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>As far as I recall, you did not. I don't know how you felt deep down, but on the surface, you seemed OK.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>据我回忆，你没有。我不知道你内心深处是什么感受，但在表面上，你看上去还好。

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:48.760
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:32:48.760 --> 00:33:04.930
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>You were a very considerate and thoughtful child, and you probably didn't want to worry us. Maybe you were upset, but you didn't want to show us.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>你是个非常体贴和懂事的孩子，你可能不想让我们担心。也许你很伤心，但你不想向我们表现出来。

00:33:04.930 --> 00:33:14.790
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>The one thing that I didn't tell you, that I wasn't honest with you, was that we were moving to the US, and we would never come back to Japan.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>我没有告诉你的一件事，我没对你诚实的一件事，就是我们要搬到美国，而且我们永远不会回到日本了。

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:17.620
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:19.510
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>This is my biggest regret.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>这是我最大的遗憾。

00:33:19.510 --> 00:33:23.230
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:32.260
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>It feels like I deceived you. I know you were upset at that time. I feel really bad about it. I'm sorry.
<v Ying-Hsi Li Through Interpreter>感觉就像我欺骗了你。我知道你当时很难过。我对此感觉真的很糟。我很抱歉。

00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:33.610
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:36.640
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[说普通话]

00:33:36.640 --> 00:34:15.170
<v Bim Adewunmi>I told her, "No need to keep apologizing. No need to be sad." Before I started these interviews, I thought either my grandma was the villain for stealing me from my dad or that my dad was for deserting me once we got to the States. And it turns out, the truth is, my grandma did take me away. And my dad could have done something to stop it, but he decided I'd be better off without him, which is so much sadder and somehow feels worse. I just feel so bad for all of them.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我告诉她，“没必要一直道歉。没必要伤心。”在开始这些访谈之前，我以为要么是祖母为了从爸爸身边偷走我是个反派，要么是爸爸因为到美国后抛弃我是个反派。事实证明，真相是，祖母确实带走了我。而爸爸本可以做点什么阻止，但他决定没有他我会更好，这更令人难过，不知怎的感觉更糟。我真为他们所有人感到难过。

00:34:15.170 --> 00:34:25.660
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Thank you for answering that. And thank you for being with me today, and I love you so much. And thank you for everything you have ever done for me, Grandma. I mean that from the bottom of my heart.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>谢谢你回答那个。也谢谢你今天陪我，我非常爱你。谢谢你为我做的一切，祖母。我从心底里那样说。

00:34:25.660 --> 00:34:29.920
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Ying-Hsi Li>[说普通话]

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:37.420
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Grandma tells the translator, "Oh, thank her, too. I am also very grateful for her."
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>祖母告诉翻译，“噢，也谢谢她。我也非常感激她。”

00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:50.920
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>It's the least I can do. Maybe I have Stockholm syndrome. I'm just kidding. [LAUGHS]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>这是我能做的最少的了。也许我有斯德哥尔摩综合征。开玩笑的。[笑]

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:57.250
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>Well, I confronted her about it recently. I was like, Grandma, did you kidnap me? Because that's what I tell people on stage.
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>好吧，我最近和她谈过这件事。我说，祖母，你绑架我了吗？因为那就是我在舞台上告诉人们的。

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:01.600
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[LAUGHTER]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>[笑声]

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:27.340
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>She was like, hey, at least because I brought you to the states, you became a comedian, a successful one. And I was like, keep going. And then she backtracked. She was like, wait. But what if you didn't find comedy? What would you have done? And I was like, well, then I guess I'd have to press charges. [LAUGHTER]
<v Atsuko Okatsuka>她说，嘿，至少因为我带你来美国，你成为了喜剧演员，一个成功的喜剧演员。然后我说，继续。然后她退缩了。她说，等等。但如果你没发现喜剧呢？你会做什么？我说，好吧，那我想我得起诉你了。[笑声]

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:42.970
<v Bim Adewunmi>Atsuko Okatsuka is a comedian. You can watch her HBO special. It's called The Intruder. She's also currently on tour with her new show. Tickets at AtsukoComedy.com. This story was produced by Ira Glass, with Miki Meek and Diane Wu.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Atsuko Okatsuka是一位喜剧演员。你可以看她的HBO特别节目。名字叫《入侵者》。她目前也在巡演她的新秀。门票在AtsukoComedy.com。这个故事由Ira Glass制作，Miki Meek和Diane Wu共同制作。

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:57.120
<v Bim Adewunmi>Coming up, a faraway planet and the alien whose spaceship just can't land there. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Bim Adewunmi>即将到来，一颗遥远的星球，以及那艘无法降落在那里的外星飞船。从芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:35:57.120 --> 00:36:13.620
<v Bim Adewunmi>It's This American Life. I'm Bim Adewunmi, sitting in for Ira Glass. Today's program, The One Place I Can't Go, all about the mental roadblocks that people set up to keep them from engaging with thorny issues. We've arrived at Act 2 of our show.
<v Bim Adewunmi>这里是《美国生活》。我是Bim Adewunmi，代班Ira Glass。今天的节目，“我无法去的地方”，全部关于人们为了避免接触棘手问题而设置的心理路障。我们到了节目的第二幕。

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:16.120
<v Bim Adewunmi>Act Two, Killing Me Softly.
<v Bim Adewunmi>第二幕，温柔地杀了我。

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:28.290
<v Bim Adewunmi>My colleague, Emmanuel Dzotsi, he has a tale about something he avoids at all costs. Even though it seems to follow him everywhere he goes. It's been like this for years. Here's Emmanuel.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我的同事Emmanuel Dzotsi有一个关于他竭尽全力回避的事物的故事。尽管它似乎随处可见。这种状况已经持续多年了。有请Emmanuel。

00:36:28.290 --> 00:36:40.700
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>What I'm about to tell you is not some funny story I tell at parties. In fact, I don't really talk about it at all. The thing I don't talk about is a song.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我接下来要告诉你的不是我在聚会上讲的什么有趣的故事。事实上，我基本不谈论它。我不谈论的那个东西是一首歌。

00:36:40.700 --> 00:37:03.740
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I don't talk about it because for over 10 years, anytime I listen to, sing, hum, or even think about it, it gets stuck in my head. And when that happens, bad things happen to me, often immediately. I've lost keys, wallets, my Social Security card after listening to this song. I lost a relationship once, got dumped after it played in a coffee shop I was in.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我不谈论它是因为十多年来，任何时候我听、唱、哼，甚至想到它，它就会在我脑海中挥之不去。当这种情况发生时，坏事就会发生在我身上，通常是立刻。听过这首歌后，我丢过钥匙、钱包、社保卡。我丢过一段感情，在一家我呆的咖啡店播放这首歌后被甩了。

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:20.640
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I'm not a superstitious person, but I truly believe it is the ultimate bad luck for me. Right before I sat down to write this, no joke, I made sure to renew my renter's insurance, just in case. That's how seriously I take this song.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我不是个迷信的人，但我真的相信这是我终极的坏运气。就在我坐下来写这个之前，不骗你，我确保更新了我的租房保险，以防万一。我就是这么认真地对待这首歌。

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:50.750
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>The first time I heard it, I was a freshman in college. YouTube suggested it to me during a morning study session for a midterm I had later that day. I remember listening to it and feeling so happy. I was having a great day, and the song was my soundtrack. I remember that feeling staying with me for hours, hearing the song in my head as I took the midterm. It was still playing in my head a few days later, when I got the results back and saw that I'd failed.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>第一次听到它时，我是大学新生。YouTube在当天晚些时候的一次期中考试早晨学习中向我推荐了它。我记得听着它感到非常开心。那天过得很棒，这首歌是我的配乐。我记得那种感觉伴随了我好几个小时，我在参加期中考试时，脑海里一直响着这首歌。几天后，当我收到成绩看到挂科时，它还在我脑子里响着。

00:37:50.750 --> 00:38:04.880
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>At the time, I didn't blame the song. I was just like, well, that's what I get for taking a class about black holes. I moved on. In fact, I added the song to a long playlist I had.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>当时，我没责怪那首歌。我只是想，好吧，这就是选修黑洞课的代价。我继续前行。事实上，我把这首歌加进了我的长播放列表里。

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:23.240
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>A few weeks later, when I was listening to music in the middle of the day after some classes, the song played, and I fell asleep while listening, slept through a work shift I had, and got fired for the first and only time in my life. I just need more sleep, I told myself. It's a coincidence.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>几周后，我在课后中午听音乐时，这首歌响了，我在听的时候睡着了，错过了工作班次，这辈子第一次也是唯一一次被开除。我告诉自己，我只是需要更多睡眠。这只是巧合。

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:55.760
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But then, months later, the next time this song came on, I found myself feeling weirdly on edge. I thought about skipping it. But just as quickly, I also thought, that's ridiculous. So I kept the song playing. Later that day, after a concert band rehearsal, I put my backpack down to help a friend move some chairs, and somebody stole it, presumably for the laptop inside. I went in the rest of the semester without a personal computer. I was freaked out.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但后来，几个月后，这首歌再次响起时，我发现自己感到莫名其妙地焦躁。我想跳过它。但转念一想，这太荒谬了。所以我让歌继续播放。那天晚些时候，音乐队排练后，我放下背包去帮朋友搬椅子，有人把它偷走了，大概是为了里面的笔记本电脑。那学期剩余时间我都没有个人电脑。我吓坏了。

00:38:55.760 --> 00:39:10.660
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>You know how in horror movies, there's always that moment where the happy couple finally realize they might not have booked a great weekend getaway at a cabin after all and that the vibes are most definitely off? That's what it felt like.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>你知道恐怖电影里总是有那种情侣终于意识到他们可能根本没有订到度假小屋，而且气氛绝对不对劲的时刻吗？就是那种感觉。

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:32.460
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>After that, even though I knew it wasn't rational, I tried to avoid the song. But it didn't work. The only bike accident I've ever been in happened after I heard it being played by a passing car. One time, it came on during a long road trip. And the next time I stopped, I noticed a nail in one of my tires.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>在那之后，虽然我知道这不理智，但我试图回避那首歌。但没用。我有史以来唯一一次自行车事故是在一辆经过的汽车播放这首歌后发生的。有一次，在一场长途驾驶中它响了。下一次停车时，我注意到轮胎上钉了一颗钉子。

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:47.900
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>When I was preparing to run my first marathon, I was putting together a playlist, and my phone played a bit of a song before I could turn it off. A few hours later, my sister called me and told me, Emmanuel, our parents are getting divorced.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>准备参加我的第一次马拉松时，我正在整理播放列表，我还没来得及关掉，手机就播放了一小段音乐。几个小时后，姐姐给我打电话说，Emmanuel，我们的父母要离婚了。

00:39:47.900 --> 00:40:11.740
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>The next morning, I forgot to bring my headphones to the race, and, for the nearly five hours it took me to run the marathon, all I could think about were my parents, their marriage, and that song, which happens to be a sappy love song, endlessly looping in my head.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>第二天早上，我忘了带耳机去比赛，在跑马拉松所需的近五个小时里，我脑海里只能想到我的父母、他们的婚姻，以及那首歌——它恰好是一首多愁善感的爱情歌曲，无休止地在我脑海里循环。

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:34.360
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I know all of this sounds delusional. A couple of years ago, I tried to convince myself, once and for all, that the song was just a song, but it backfired spectacularly. I decided to play it every day for a month. I'd wake up, play the song, and then walk around the rest of the day telling myself not to worry.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我知道这一切听起来像妄想。几年前，我试着彻底说服自己，这首歌只是一首歌，但它适得其反。我决定连续一个月每天播放它。我会醒来，播放这首歌，然后在那天余下的时间里走来走去告诉自己不要担心。

00:40:34.360 --> 00:40:55.210
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>When nothing bad happened for three days, I felt relieved. And then, on the fourth day, I suffered a freak wrist injury, which plagued me for nine months. To make matters worse, because I'd played it so much, Spotify kept pushing me the song for months after the accident, because it thought I wanted to hear it.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>当三天里什么坏事都没发生时，我感到如释重负。然后，在第四天，我遭遇了一次离奇的手腕受伤，折磨了我九个月。更糟糕的是，因为我播放得太多，事故发生后的好几个月，Spotify一直在向我推送这首歌，因为它以为我想听它。

00:40:55.210 --> 00:41:16.990
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>That year was, by far, the worst year of my 20s. My hair, which, until that point, was decently robust, started falling out aggressively. My long-time family cat died. I went through a massive breakup. My professional life was a mess. And I got COVID right before Christmas, so I spent my first-ever Christmas completely alone.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>那一年是我二十多岁里最糟糕的一年。我的头发，在那之前还是相当浓密的，开始疯狂掉落。陪伴我多年的家庭猫死了。我经历了一次巨大的分手。我的职业生涯一团糟。圣诞节前夕我感染了新冠，所以我独自一人度过了有史以来第一个圣诞节。

00:41:16.990 --> 00:41:32.410
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>And it pushed me to do something I should have done years earlier. I added the group that sings the song to my do-not-play list on Spotify, which is a shame, because I really like the song. It is good with a capital G.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>这促使我做了一件我应该早几年做的事。我把这首歌的演唱组合加入了我的Spotify禁止播放列表，这很遗憾，因为我真的很喜欢这首歌。它是那种“大G”意义上的好歌。

00:41:32.410 --> 00:42:07.300
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>It is one of those R&B disco ballads from the '70s that is so ridiculously happy, so positive, it makes you wonder if the '70s were the most optimistic Americans have ever been or if things were actually so bleak artists had to make songs like this one. But yeah, no, I will not be playing this song or naming it. It's poison to me.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>它是那种70年代的R&B迪斯科情歌之一，如此滑稽地快乐，如此积极，让你怀疑70年代是否是美国人有史以来最乐观的时期，还是当时的情况真的很惨淡，以至于艺术家们不得不创作这样的歌曲。但是的，不，我不会播放这首歌或说出它的名字。它是我的毒药。

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:31.660
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>A skeptical friend of mine pointed out that yes, bad things happen when I hear this song, but don't bad things happen to all of us all the time? Maybe I just noticed them when the song plays. He suggested I try to keep track of every unfortunate event I encounter without the song so I could see, once and for all, there was nothing to this.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我的一位怀疑论者朋友指出，是的，当我听到这首歌时坏事发生了，但坏事不是一直发生在所有人身上吗？也许我只是在歌响的时候注意到了它们。他建议我尝试记录下我遇到的每一件不幸事件，不听这首歌，这样我就能一劳永逸地看出这根本没什么。

00:42:31.660 --> 00:42:57.850
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>And so I did it. I listed every tiny thing over the course of a couple of weeks, and I was surprised by how many there were, even if they were small. I cut myself on a metal can, accidentally got bleach on one of my favorite T-shirts, got into a never-ending back and forth with my health insurance over almost 20 claims they didn't want to pay. I spent half of a family wedding in the ER because a guest passed out at the reception.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>于是我做了。我在几周内列出了每一件微小的事情，我很惊讶竟然有这么多，即使它们都很小。我不小心被金属罐割伤，不小心把漂白剂弄到我最喜欢的一件T恤上，和我的健康保险公司在近20个他们不想支付的理赔单上进行无休止的往来。我在参加一场家庭婚礼时，有一半时间都在急诊室，因为有个客人在招待会上晕倒了。

00:42:57.850 --> 00:43:17.440
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I know I'm supposed to look at that list and feel freed, feel finally able to listen to that song whenever I want, but-- don't judge me-- all it did was convince me that lousy stuff happens to me a lot. So why would I risk playing this song and making it worse?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我知道我本该看着那份清单感到解脱，感到终于能随时随地听那首歌了，但是——别评判我——它所做的只是让我确信糟糕的事情经常发生在我身上。所以我为什么要冒着播放这首歌让事情变得更糟的风险呢？

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:50.940
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I have nothing else like this in my life. I don't believe in astrology. I don't believe in luck. I don't believe that misfortune is God punishing you for your bad deeds. I know it's entirely possible that when I hear that tune I freak out in a way that causes unpleasant things to happen to me. But this I believe. I have to stay away from this song.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我的生活中没有其他这样的东西了。我不相信占星术。我不相信运气。我不相信不幸是上帝对你恶行的惩罚。我知道完全有可能当我听到那段旋律时，我以一种导致不愉快事情发生的方式吓坏了自己。但我相信这一点。我必须远离这首歌。

00:43:50.940 --> 00:44:15.720
<v Bim Adewunmi>Emmanuel Dzotsi is a producer on our show. Right after working on the final draft of this piece, he went for a run in the woods, got chased by a loose dog, and fell, bruising his ribs and busting his ankle. As of this taping, he's also home sick with strep throat.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Emmanuel Dzotsi是我们节目的制作人。在完成这篇稿件的最终定稿后，他去树林里跑步，被一只没拴绳的狗追赶，摔倒了，擦伤了肋骨并挫伤了脚踝。在这次录音时，他还因为链球菌性咽喉炎在家病休。

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:18.060
<v Bim Adewunmi>Act Three, Lost in Space.
<v Bim Adewunmi>第三幕，迷失在太空中。

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:35.180
<v Bim Adewunmi>This last story is about someone who spent her childhood craving a world that she could not find on Earth, so, as an adult, she just created one. And it was perfect until she became the one person who couldn't go there. Lilly Sullivan has the story.
<v Bim Adewunmi>这最后一个故事关于一个童年渴望在地球上找不到的世界的人，所以，成年后，她创造了一个。那曾经是完美的，直到她成了唯一一个无法进入那里的人。Lilly Sullivan有这个故事。

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:41.820
<v Lilly Sullivan>First let me tell you about the world she created. It's called The Locked Tomb. It starts on an isolated planet.
<v Lilly Sullivan>首先让我告诉你她创造的世界。它被称为《锁墓》。它始于一个孤立的星球。

00:44:41.820 --> 00:44:51.400
<v Tamsyn Muir>And it is a fundamentally dying monastery of a planet, where there are pretty much only three people under 30.
<v Tamsyn Muir>那是一个从根本上垂死的修道院星球，基本上只有三个30岁以下的人。

00:44:51.400 --> 00:45:09.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>This is Tamsyn Muir. She's the author of this series of science fantasy books, which begin on that sad planet. But they are also some of the most breezily gay books I've ever read. The blurb on the cover of the first book basically just says, "Lesbian necromancers in space." So I was in.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这是Tamsyn Muir。她是这个科学奇幻系列书籍的作者，这些书始于那个悲伤的星球。但它们也是我读过的最随性、最酷儿的书籍之一。第一本书封底的简介基本上只是说，“太空中的女同性恋通灵师”。所以我进去了。

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:40.840
<v Lilly Sullivan>Reading the first one was unlike anything I've ever read. Here's first line. "In the myriadic year of our Lord, the 10,000th year of the king undying, the kindly prince of death, Gideon Nav packed her sword, her shoes, and her dirty magazines. And she escaped from the house of the night." Gideon is a Cavalier, which is kind of like a knight, but she's not like a classic knight. She refers to people as douchebags, and she's constantly saying things like, "That's what she said."
<v Lilly Sullivan>读第一本时的体验不同于我读过的任何东西。这是第一行。“在主宰的万世之年，不朽之王、仁慈的死亡之君执政的第一万年，Gideon Nav带上了她的剑、鞋子和她的色情杂志。她逃离了夜晚之屋。” Gideon是一名骑士（Cavalier），有点像骑士，但她不像那种经典骑士。她称呼别人为混蛋，而且经常说像“她就是这么说的”之类的话。

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:59.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>Unlike most queer books, there's barely any sex or romance or making out. The words "gay," "lesbian," "queer" never even show up. People are queer, but it's more like their personalities, their humor. It's just understood. And I'll just say, I'm sure these books aren't for everyone, but I love them.
<v Lilly Sullivan>与大多数酷儿书籍不同，这里几乎没有性、浪漫或接吻。词汇“同性恋”、“女同性恋”、“酷儿”甚至从未出现过。人们是酷儿，但这更多是关于他们的个性、幽默。这只是心领神会。我只想说，我相信这些书并不适合每个人，但我爱它们。

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:26.320
<v Lilly Sullivan>I read the first book one summer a few years ago. I was on vacation with a few friends, and we all got pulled into it at the same time. And it was like we all just fell into this kind of imaginary space that Tamsyn had created, kind of youthful and very gay, in a fun rather than oppressive way, which, Tamsyn says, is exactly what she'd hoped for. Tamsyn says she wrote this book for her 17-year-old self, who really could have used books and worlds like this back then.
<v Lilly Sullivan>几年前的一个夏天我读了第一本。我和几个朋友度假，我们同时被吸引进去了。就像我们都掉进了Tamsyn创造的那种虚构空间，青春且非常酷儿，是以一种有趣而非压抑的方式，Tamsyn说，这正是她所希望的。Tamsyn说她为17岁的自己写了这本书，当年的她真的很需要这样的书和世界。

00:46:26.320 --> 00:46:53.440
<v Tamsyn Muir>Just ways to be queer that weren't inherently sad. The very mainstream LGBQT stuff, it was miserable. And I was already miserable, because if I wanted to read gay literature at the time, it was always like, my sad, coming-out summer. And yeah, I didn't want to read my sad coming-out summer for the umpty-umpth time. I'd read it. It was good the first 50 times.
<v Tamsyn Muir>就是那些非本质上悲伤的酷儿方式。非常主流的LGBT内容，都很悲惨。我已经很悲惨了，因为如果当时我想读酷儿文学，总是像：我悲伤的出柜之夏。是的，我不想第N次读我的悲伤出柜之夏。我读过了。前50次读起来还不错。

00:46:53.440 --> 00:47:12.290
<v Tamsyn Muir>What I really wanted was a big set piece fight. I wanted to read about girls doing big set piece fights. I mean, that was the era of The Matrix, and I'd also just gotten super into Hong Kong kung fu cinema. And all I did was imagine big kung fu fights.
<v Tamsyn Muir>我真正想要的是一场大型场景战斗。我想读关于女孩们进行大型场景战斗的故事。我的意思是，那是《黑客帝国》的时代，我也刚对香港功夫电影着迷。我做的全部事就是想象大型功夫战斗。

00:47:12.290 --> 00:47:35.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>Tamsyn ended up dropping out of high school. She says she wasn't a good student. She'd been put in the class for the, quote, "stupid kids." She said she spent most of high school dyeing her hair, shaving it ways nobody liked, sitting in the back of the class, her eyes glazed over, daydreaming about big martial arts fights. She came out in high school, in a small conservative New Zealand suburb, a different era.
<v Lilly Sullivan>Tamsyn最终从高中辍学。她说她不是个好学生。她被分进了所谓的“傻瓜班”。她说她大部分高中时光都在染发，剪成没人喜欢的样子，坐在教室后面，目光呆滞，做着大型武术打斗的白日梦。她在高中出柜，在一个保守的小型新西兰郊区，一个不同的时代。

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:50.150
<v Tamsyn Muir>And one of my best friends stopped talking to me immediately. And I don't even blame her. I think a lot of people forget how dangerous and grotesque being gay was presented back at the time.
<v Tamsyn Muir>我最好的朋友之一立刻停止和我说话。我甚至不怪她。我觉得很多人都忘了那个时代对同性恋的呈现有多危险和荒诞。

00:47:50.150 --> 00:48:01.970
<v Lilly Sullivan>She was bullied, beaten up. And to escape, she wrote stories, which is not such a surprise given where she was hanging out online. It's a place she stumbled on when she was about 11.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她被欺负、殴打。为了逃避，她写故事，考虑到她在网上混迹的地方，这并不奇怪。那是她11岁左右偶然发现的地方。

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:31.880
<v Tamsyn Muir>My dad had been on ARPANET, so was that connected with old-school internet. And the internet of 1996 was just a wild frontier. That was cowboy territory. Anything was happening. And I just wanted to look up cool pictures of Sailor Moon or go on ICQ and talk about animals and gargoyles, and I found fanfiction, and I didn't look back.
<v Tamsyn Muir>我爸爸曾参与过ARPANET，所以那是与老派互联网连接的。1996年的互联网只是一个狂野的边疆。那是牛仔领地。任何事情都在发生。我只是想查找《美少女战士》的酷图，或者在ICQ上谈论动物和石像鬼，我发现了同人小说，从此一去不回。

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:54.380
<v Lilly Sullivan>Fanfiction, a whole world of people equally obsessed with stories and sci-fi, to the point they were going online to write their own versions of the stories they loved, X-Files, Lord of the Rings. They fix the things they feel the author got wrong. There's tons of shipping, that is, people deciding which characters should have ended up together and rewriting it so that they do.
<v Lilly Sullivan>同人小说，一个沉迷于故事和科幻的群体，以至于他们上网编写自己所爱故事的版本，比如《X档案》、《指环王》。他们修复他们觉得作者写错的地方。这里有大量的配对（shipping），也就是说，人们决定哪些角色应该在一起，并改写它使之成真。

00:48:54.380 --> 00:49:09.590
<v Lilly Sullivan>None of this is for money, for the most part. It's just for fun. Tamsyn was just a kid, but she starts publishing stories there, stuff she never would have taken to school. It's the first place she ever published anything, the first place she ever really showed anyone her work.
<v Lilly Sullivan>大部分情况下，这些都不是为了钱。只是为了好玩。Tamsyn当时还是个孩子，但她开始在那里发表故事，那些她绝不会带去学校的东西。这是她发表任何作品的第一个地方，她真正向任何人展示作品的第一个地方。

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:30.560
<v Tamsyn Muir>And I think one of the reasons I got into it initially was because it felt so illegal and faintly naughty. Fanfiction, especially back then, was like the dark web for nerds. You read a story, and the author's note immediately says, please don't sue me. It's like punk for dorks.
<v Tamsyn Muir>我认为我最初参与其中的原因之一是因为它感觉如此非法且隐约有些调皮。同人小说，尤其是在当时，就像是书呆子的暗网。你读一个故事，作者说明里立刻写着，请不要起诉我。这就像书呆子的朋克。

00:49:30.560 --> 00:49:58.940
<v Lilly Sullivan>Punk for dorks, in other words, her people. A lot of people think of fanfic as lowbrow, embarrassing. A lot of big sci-fi fantasy writers slam it. George R. R. Martin, who wrote Game of Thrones, has said it's copyright infringement, a violation of intellectual property. Anne Rice, who wrote Interview With the Vampire, has famously objected to people writing fic about her stories, because who wouldn't want to ship Brad Pitt and Tom Cruise?
<v Lilly Sullivan>书呆子的朋克，换句话说，她的人。很多人认为同人小说是低俗、尴尬的。许多大牌科幻奇幻作家抨击它。《权力的游戏》作者George R. R. Martin曾说这是侵犯版权，侵犯知识产权。《夜访吸血鬼》作者Anne Rice曾名声在外地反对人们写关于她故事的同人，因为谁不想把Brad Pitt和Tom Cruise配对呢？

00:49:58.940 --> 00:50:22.040
<v Lilly Sullivan>There are some writers who, like Tamsyn, write fic before going mainstream. But once they make it mainstream, they might delete their accounts, delete all the things they wrote pre-mainstream days. Not Tamsyn. Tamsyn still loves these worlds. When the first book in the series came out, Gideon the Ninth, it was a big deal, nominated for the biggest sci-fi awards.
<v Lilly Sullivan>有一些作家像Tamsyn一样在进入主流之前写同人。但一旦他们进入主流，他们可能会删除账号，删除在主流之前写的那些东西。不是Tamsyn。Tamsyn仍然热爱这些世界。该系列的第一本书《九号基甸》（Gideon the Ninth）问世时，引起了轰动，获得了科幻最高奖项提名。

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:48.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>A review in The New York Times called it "meticulous and moody, haunted by difficult and complex feelings in a wasted universe." And in all this excitement about the book, Tamsyn heard about some buzz from the place where she'd always felt so comfortable, one of the main fanfiction sites, AO3, or Archive of Our Own. One day, she saw something she'd never seen before. People had started writing fanfic about her work.
<v Lilly Sullivan>《纽约时报》的一篇评论称其为“严谨而忧郁，被荒凉宇宙中复杂而困难的情感所困扰”。在所有关于这本书的兴奋中，Tamsyn听到了一些来自她一直感到舒适的地方的嗡嗡声，主要的同人小说网站之一，AO3或“我们的档案”（Archive of Our Own）。一天，她看到了她从未见过的事物。人们开始写关于她作品的同人小说。

00:50:48.050 --> 00:51:01.060
<v Tamsyn Muir>And I think I saw there were eight fanfics. And I was like, oh, oh, oh. I'm feeling a lot of different emotions, and I have to run away right now. I cannot look at even what these titles are.
<v Tamsyn Muir>我想我看到有八篇同人小说。我就想，噢，噢，噢。我感觉到了许多不同的情绪，我必须立刻跑开。我连这些标题是什么都不能看。

00:51:01.060 --> 00:51:01.510
<v Lilly Sullivan>Why?
<v Lilly Sullivan>为什么？

00:51:01.510 --> 00:51:09.910
<v Tamsyn Muir>You know inherently starting out in fan fiction that you are not allowed to read it if it's for your own creation.
<v Tamsyn Muir>你本质上知道，一旦开始写同人小说，如果是为了你自己的创作，你是不允许阅读它的。

00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:11.330
<v Lilly Sullivan>What are the reasons?
<v Lilly Sullivan>原因是什么？

00:51:11.330 --> 00:51:41.020
<v Tamsyn Muir>So I think one of the main things is that you worry that if you read fan fiction and then you write anything that mimics anything that somebody has written about your property, you just committed a huge cardinal sin against your own fandom. You just stole candy from the world's most important baby. You ripped off your own fans. You are scum. Even if you hadn't meant to, there is no way that you can prove that you didn't nick off somebody else's ideas.
<v Tamsyn Muir>所以我认为主要原因是你会担心，如果你读了同人小说，然后你写了任何模仿别人关于你的财产所写的东西，你就犯下了针对你自己粉丝群体的一个巨大罪孽。你刚从世界上最重要的宝宝那里偷了糖果。你剽窃了你自己的粉丝。你是渣滓。即使你并非有意，你也没有办法证明你不是偷了别人的想法。

00:51:41.020 --> 00:52:04.880
<v Lilly Sullivan>Tamsyn says that writers in general are told pretty firmly to not read fanfic about their work. There needs to be no question of where their ideas come from. Earlier this year, a fanfic writer sued the Tolkien Estate and Amazon Studios for $250 million, alleging they'd ripped off his storyline for The Lord of the Rings prequel. The Tolkien Trust countersued for copyright infringement.
<v Lilly Sullivan>Tamsyn说作家通常被强烈建议不要读关于他们作品的同人。必须对想法的来源没有疑问。今年早些时候，一名同人小说作家起诉托尔金基金会和亚马逊工作室，索赔2.5亿美元，称他们剽窃了他的《指环王》前传情节。托尔金基金会反诉版权侵权。

00:52:04.880 --> 00:52:25.580
<v Lilly Sullivan>If you look up Tamsyn's work on the big fic sites today, there are thousands of entries, people writing, interacting, playing around in the world of The Locked Tomb. Tamsyn can't participate in any of it. And it kills her because, again, these are her people. And now that it's about her own books even more so her people.
<v Lilly Sullivan>如果你今天在大型同人网站上查找Tamsyn的作品，有成千上万的条目，人们在写作、互动、在《锁墓》的世界里玩耍。Tamsyn不能参与其中任何一个。这让她很难受，因为，再次强调，这些是她的人。既然是关于她自己的书，就更是她的人了。

00:52:25.580 --> 00:52:44.420
<v Lilly Sullivan>Not only do they like the same books, but they are devoted to her characters, her humor, the entire fantastical sci-fi universe that lives in her head. So the people who loved it were like a custom-made group of people who could not possibly have interests more tailored to Tamsyn's own brain, all milling around in one spot.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们不仅喜欢同样的书，而且他们致力于她的角色、她的幽默，整个活在她脑海里的幻想科幻宇宙。所以那些爱它的人就像一个量身定做的群体，不可能有比这更符合Tamsyn大脑兴趣的人了，全部聚集在一个点上。

00:52:44.420 --> 00:52:47.900
<v Lilly Sullivan>Do you have questions about what's going on in--
<v Lilly Sullivan>你对那里发生的事情有疑问吗——

00:52:47.900 --> 00:53:08.190
<v Tamsyn Muir>Oh, so many. I want to know everything. Because fandom is so deeply about shipping, whose faces do you want to make mush together? I'm just sitting here, eating popcorn, being like, who do you want to make mush? Come on. Who are you making kiss? I want to figure it out.
<v Tamsyn Muir>噢，太多了。我想知道一切。因为粉丝圈深深关于配对，你想把谁的脸揉在一起？我就坐在这，吃着爆米花，想着，你想让谁揉在一起？来吧。你在让谁接吻？我想弄明白。

00:53:08.190 --> 00:53:30.230
<v Tamsyn Muir>And then, when I realized that I wasn't going to be able to do that, I'm the kind of person who doesn't do anything by halves. I'm all in or I'm all out. And I realized then that just being a writer, I kind of didn't want to interact with anyone at all. I was all about that hermit lifestyle.
<v Tamsyn Muir>然后，当我意识到我无法做到这一点时，我属于那种凡事不做半途的人。我要么全心投入，要么完全退出。我那时意识到作为作家，我其实并不想和任何人互动。我全心全意投入了那种隐士生活。

00:53:30.230 --> 00:53:39.200
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh, yeah. I mean, it seems like it must be hard to avoid. You're just a couple keystrokes away just to quickly peek. Is it ever hard?
<v Lilly Sullivan>噢，是的。我的意思是，这看起来一定很难避免。你只需要几下键盘就能快速偷看。这真的很难吗？

00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:59.630
<v Tamsyn Muir>It's impossible. That's why most of the internet is banned. I have a beautiful program called Freedom, and I have banned myself from looking pretty much anywhere, because I know I would go look, I would go look at everything. So I had to be sensible, and I've banned myself from fandom.
<v Tamsyn Muir>这是不可能的。这就是为什么互联网大部分被禁了。我有一个名为“Freedom”的漂亮程序，我禁止自己去看任何地方，因为我知道我会去看，我会去看一切。所以我必须明智，我禁止了自己进入粉丝圈。

00:53:59.630 --> 00:54:01.370
<v Lilly Sullivan>You had to exile yourself.
<v Lilly Sullivan>你不得不流放自己。

00:54:01.370 --> 00:54:05.540
<v Tamsyn Muir>I have exiled myself. I have left. I have gone into the West.
<v Tamsyn Muir>我已经流放了自己。我离开了。我已经前往西方。

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:08.150
<v Lilly Sullivan>That seems like it would have its own kind of melancholy.
<v Lilly Sullivan>那似乎会有它自己那种忧郁。

00:54:08.150 --> 00:54:12.950
<v Tamsyn Muir>Yeah. Yeah, it's lonely. You know how I said I wanted no loneliness? I got loneliness.
<v Tamsyn Muir>是的。是的，很孤独。你知道我说过我不想要孤独吗？我得到了孤独。

00:54:12.950 --> 00:54:13.490
<v Lilly Sullivan>Wow.
<v Lilly Sullivan>哇。

00:54:13.490 --> 00:54:26.600
<v Tamsyn Muir>Yeah. It's a particular kind of melancholy that I didn't think I would ever experience.
<v Tamsyn Muir>是的。那是一种我从没想过我会经历的特定忧郁。

00:54:26.600 --> 00:54:45.440
<v Lilly Sullivan>Talking to her, somehow I couldn't help but tell her what it was like being in that world among her fans. I told her about that summer vacation I spent listening to her book together with friends, how we bought a little boat and named it Ianthe. It was one of the characters in the books, a villain. People online call her a Draco in leather pants.
<v Lilly Sullivan>与她交谈，不知怎的，我不禁告诉她置身于她粉丝的世界中是什么感觉。我告诉她那个我与朋友们一起听她书的暑假，我们买了一艘小船，命名为Ianthe。它是书中的角色之一，一个反派。网民称她为“皮裤里的Draco”。

00:54:45.440 --> 00:54:51.590
<v Tamsyn Muir>That boat's going to sink. Why would you name your boat Ianthe? Your boat's cursed.
<v Tamsyn Muir>那船会沉的。为什么要把你的船命名为Ianthe？你的船被诅咒了。

00:54:51.590 --> 00:54:56.720
<v Lilly Sullivan>We would jump off of it into the lake and yell "Ianthe" as we were jumping into the lake.
<v Lilly Sullivan>我们会从船上跳进湖里，在我们跳进湖里时大喊“Ianthe”。

00:54:56.720 --> 00:55:00.470
<v Tamsyn Muir>Sorry. I don't mean to roast you about the boat. It's just, that's the greatest thing I've ever heard.
<v Tamsyn Muir>抱歉。我不是故意要嘲笑你的船。只是，那是我听过的最棒的事情。

00:55:00.470 --> 00:55:07.810
<v Lilly Sullivan>Fandom around her books, speaking from experience, it's really fun. This is what she's missing.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她书周围的粉丝圈，以我的经验来说，真的很有趣。这就是她错过的。

00:55:07.810 --> 00:55:13.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>It's like you created this whole world that would be perfect for you and perfect for your 17-year-old self.
<v Lilly Sullivan>就像你创造了这个整个世界，对你来说是完美的，对你17岁的自己也是完美的。

00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:26.440
<v Tamsyn Muir>It's your own world, and you can't even post there. You've created the perfect thing for you, and you're on the outside of it. You've been monkey's pawed. You've created the perfect fandom, and you can never walk inside it.
<v Tamsyn Muir>那是你自己的世界，而你甚至不能在那里发帖。你创造了对自己完美的东西，却站在它的外面。你被“猴爪”了。你创造了完美的粉丝圈，却永远不能走进它。

00:55:26.440 --> 00:55:28.420
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah, yeah.
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的，是的。

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:36.730
<v Tamsyn Muir>You know how Frodo says that he saved the Shire, but not for himself? It's like that, except Frodo wasn't blocking his own name on Twitter.
<v Tamsyn Muir>你知道Frodo说他拯救了夏尔，但不是为了他自己？就是那样，除了Frodo没有在Twitter上屏蔽他自己的名字。

00:55:36.730 --> 00:56:06.080
<v Lilly Sullivan>She's also come to accept, this is their party. If she arrives, it's like the teacher showing up and trying to hang with the students. Her very presence means that no one can act normal. And she understands that, but it's OK, she says. She gets to spend plenty of time in the universe of The Locked Tomb. She's still at work writing the end of the series for the rest of us to enjoy without her.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她也接受了，这是他们的派对。如果她出现，就像老师出现试图和学生打成一片。她的存在意味着没人能表现正常。她理解这一点，但这没关系，她说。她有足够的时间在《锁墓》的宇宙里消磨。她仍然在写这个系列的结尾，让我们这些人在没有她的情况下享受。

00:56:06.080 --> 00:56:46.890
<v Bim Adewunmi>Lilly Sullivan is a producer on our show. The third book in Tamsyn Muir's Locked Tomb series, Nona the Ninth, just came out in paperback.
<v Bim Adewunmi>Lilly Sullivan是我们节目的制作人。Tamsyn Muir的《锁墓》系列第三本书《九号Nona》刚刚出了平装本。

00:56:46.890 --> 00:57:11.460
<v Bim Adewunmi>Today's program was produced by Diane Wu and edited by our senior editor David Kestenbaum. The people who put together our show today include Jane Ackermann, James Bennett II, Phia Bennin, Zoe Chace, Sean Cole, Michael Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Cassie Howley, Valerie Kipnis, Seth Lind, Tobin Low, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Alyssa Shipp, Alix Spiegel, Christopher Swetala, and Matt Tierney.
<v Bim Adewunmi>今天的节目由Diane Wu制作，资深编辑David Kestenbaum编辑。今天整理我们节目的人员包括Jane Ackermann、James Bennett II、Phia Bennin、Zoe Chace、Sean Cole、Michael Comite、Aviva DeKornfeld、Cassie Howley、Valerie Kipnis、Seth Lind、Tobin Low、Stowe Nelson、Katherine Rae Mondo、Nadia Reiman、Ryan Rumery、Alyssa Shipp、Alix Spiegel、Christopher Swetala和Matt Tierney。

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:20.760
<v Bim Adewunmi>Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry. Special thanks today to Noriko Meek, Caro Perny, Taylor Britton, and Mikafui Dzotsi.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我们的管理编辑是Sarah Abdurrahman。我们的执行编辑是Emanuele Berry。今天特别感谢Noriko Meek、Caro Perny、Taylor Britton和Mikafui Dzotsi。

00:57:20.760 --> 00:57:33.240
<v Bim Adewunmi>Interpretation for act one by Siyi Chen and Yukiko Hata, with research help from Zeyi Yang, Hisae Kawamori, Kiku Matsuo, Karin Jeffery, and Japanese voice over by Toshiji Takeshima.
<v Bim Adewunmi>第一幕的翻译由Siyi Chen和Yukiko Hata完成，研究协助来自Zeyi Yang、Hisae Kawamori、Kiku Matsuo、Karin Jeffery，日语配音由Toshiji Takeshima完成。

00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:46.540
<v Bim Adewunmi>Our website, thisamericanlife.org, where you can stream our archive of over 800 episodes for absolutely free. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我们的网站thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以免费流媒体播放我们超过800集的档案。《美国生活》由公共广播交换平台PRX分发到公共广播电台。

00:57:46.540 --> 00:58:05.380
<v Bim Adewunmi>Thanks, as always, to my boss Ira Glass. He's recently started coaching a team of amateur bobsledders. The vibe is supposed to be super relaxed. No one's keeping score. But he's also really competitive, wants his team members to get the fastest times. You can hear how conflicted he is each time he shoves them down the track.
<v Bim Adewunmi>感谢一如既往的老板Ira Glass。他最近开始指导一支业余雪橇队。氛围本应该是超级放松的。没人记分。但他真的很有竞争力，希望他的队员拿到最快成绩。每次他把他们推下赛道时，你都能听到他有多纠结。

00:58:05.380 --> 00:58:10.150
<v Ira Glass>Hurry. Have fun. Ahh!
<v Ira Glass>快点。玩得开心。啊！

00:58:10.150 --> 00:58:15.150
<v Bim Adewunmi>I'm Bim Adewunmi, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我是Bim Adewunmi，下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。
